      source={{ text: 'Deloitte survey of 3,200+ business leaders in 24 countries, press release by Deloitte Middle East, 4 June 2026. The size of the Middle East sample is not published.', href: DELOITTE }}
    />

    <p>The gap between expecting a return and getting one is the subject of this guide. The same
       Deloitte survey found that 84% of organisations had not yet redesigned jobs or workflows
       around AI. Boston Consulting Group found in October 2024 that 74% of companies had yet to
       show tangible value from AI, and that the leaders put about 70% of their effort into people
       and processes rather than technology (<a href={BCG}>BCG, 1,000 executives in 59 countries</a>).</p>
    <p>Put simply, a return is decided before the tool is bought. It depends on which task you
       pick, how well you understand it, and whether you counted every cost. We look at the other
       reasons in <a href="/learn/why-ai-projects-fail/">why AI projects fail, and how to avoid it</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="measure">
    <h2>How do you measure the work before AI?</h2>
    <p>You measure it by watching the work and timing it, step by step, as it is done today. A
       guess at how long a job takes is almost always wrong, because people forget the waiting,
       the checking and the chasing.</p>
    <p>For each task you want to improve, write down:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>How often it happens:</strong> times a day, a week or a month.</li>
      <li><strong>How long each step takes:</strong> timed, not estimated, including hand-offs and
          waiting.</li>
      <li><strong>Who does it:</strong> the role, and roughly what an hour of that role costs you.</li>
      <li><strong>How often it goes wrong:</strong> rework, corrections and complaints.</li>
      <li><strong>What the step needs:</strong> the data it reads and where that data lives.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Multiply frequency by time and you have the hours a month the task takes now. That is the
       ceiling on what any tool can save. RAND's study of failed AI projects named a misunderstood
       problem as the most common cause (<a href={RAND}>RAND, August 2024</a>), and timing the work
       is the cheapest way to understand the problem.</p>
    <p>Not every task needs AI to get faster. If the rules are fixed and the inputs are clean, plain
       automation is cheaper and safer; we explain the difference in
       <a href="/learn/ai-vs-automation/">AI vs automation: which does your business need?</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="costs">
    <h2>Which costs do people forget?</h2>
    <p>The subscription price is usually the smallest cost. These are the hidden costs of AI tools
       that turn a promising sum into a loss:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Setup:</strong> connecting the tool to your systems, setting it up for your work
          and testing it.</li>
      <li><strong>Training:</strong> the hours your people spend learning it, and the slower weeks
          while they get used to it.</li>
      <li><strong>Data clean-up:</strong> AI reads what you give it. Scattered spreadsheets, old
          records and missing fields have to be fixed first.</li>
      <li><strong>Human review:</strong> someone checking the output, every time or on a sample. This
          cost never goes away for work where a mistake matters.</li>
      <li><strong>Running both:</strong> the old way and the new way side by side until you trust
          the new one.</li>
      <li><strong>Upkeep:</strong> changes when your process, prices or forms change, and someone who
          owns the tool inside the business.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Leave any of these out and the payback month moves further away than the plan says.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="payback">
    <h2>How do you work out the payback month?</h2>
    <p>The AI payback period is the one-off cost divided by the net saving each month. The net
       saving is the value of the hours saved minus everything you pay every month to keep the tool
       running, including review.</p>
    <p><strong>Payback in months = one-off costs ÷ (monthly value of hours saved minus monthly
       running costs)</strong></p>
    <p>For a return over a set period, the usual AI ROI formula is: value gained minus total cost,
       divided by total cost. Use the same period for both, and count every cost from the list above.</p>
    <p><strong>An example with round, illustrative numbers.</strong> These figures are made up to
       show the method. They are not a client result, a price we charge or a saving anyone should
       expect.</p>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Example item</th><th scope="col">Illustrative figure</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A month-end report, timed today</td><td>10 hours a month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hours a tool would really save</td><td>6 hours a month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost of an hour of that role</td><td>AED 100</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Value of hours saved</td><td>AED 600 a month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Subscription</td><td>AED 200 a month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Checking the output (1 hour)</td><td>AED 100 a month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Net saving</td><td>AED 300 a month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Setup, training and data clean-up, once</td><td>AED 3,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Payback</td><td>3,000 ÷ 300 = 10 months</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>In this example, the first year's return is small: AED 7,200 of value against AED 6,600 of
       total cost, about 9%. The second year looks much better, because the one-off costs are paid.
       That is a fair answer, and much more useful than a vendor's promise. If the payback runs past
       the life of the process, or past the next system change, the answer is no.</p>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-roi-payback"
      from="Month-end report"
      to="Payback month"
      fact="Example: 6 hours a month"
      hub="Time each step"
      hubNote="hours, cost, payback"
