  <section id="real">
    <h2>When are saved hours not real savings?</h2>
    <p>Saved hours are only worth money if something changes because of them. Six hours a month
       spread across six people, thirty minutes at a time, rarely turns into anything you can count.</p>
    <p>Saved time becomes real when it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>lets the team take on more work without hiring;</li>
      <li>removes overtime, temporary staff or outsourced work you pay for now;</li>
      <li>moves people to work that brings in revenue or keeps customers;</li>
      <li>cuts errors that cost you refunds, penalties or lost clients.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Be honest about which of these applies before you count the saving. Also watch for time that
       simply moves: if a tool drafts replies in seconds but a manager now spends longer checking
       them, the saving belongs to one desk and the cost to another.</p>
    <p>Sometimes the bigger return comes from no tool at all. When a process has too many exceptions or
       nobody owns it, fixing the process first is cheaper, and a tool added later earns more. Our
       guide on <a href="/learn/where-ai-helps-your-business/">how to find where AI helps your
       business</a> shows how to spot those steps.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="recommendation">
    <h2>What should a recommendation tell you before you spend?</h2>
    <p>Before you spend on any AI tool, the advice you are given should answer five questions in
       writing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Effort:</strong> how much work it takes to put in place.</li>
      <li><strong>A cost range:</strong> one-off and monthly, including the hidden costs above.</li>
      <li><strong>Hours saved each month:</strong> based on the timed work, not a brochure.</li>
      <li><strong>Payback:</strong> the month it pays for itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Where a person stays in charge:</strong> which decisions the tool must never make
          alone.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>That is what every recommendation in <a href="/consulting/">our AI Readiness Assessment</a>
       gives you. Each team gets one of four verdicts with the reasons behind it: AI now, Automate
       without AI, Fix the process first, or Leave as it is. Advice that cannot name the payback
       month, or never says where AI should not be used, is a sales pitch.</p>
  </section>

